Formal languages, coded by expressions, form a partially effectively given neighbourhood system.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`SsysX_default`).** The default presented language is the universal language of all finite Boolean words.

**Definition (`boolNat`).** A Boolean value is represented by zero for false and one for true.

**Theorem (`boolNat_eq_one_iff`).** The zero–one test bool nat returns one exactly when the mathematical condition encoded in its definition holds.

*Proof.* Separate the possible zero–one values occurring in bool nat eq one iff; the value one survives precisely in the case described by the defining predicate. ∎

**Theorem (`boolNat_zero_one`).** For every input, bool nat takes one of the two values zero and one.

*Proof.* Each Boolean-valued component of bool nat zero one is zero or one, and the defining Boolean combination preserves that range. ∎

**Definition (`encodeListBool`).** The coding function list bool assigns its finite list, expression, pair, or tagged value a natural-number index by pairing and list coding.

**Definition (`SExpr.encode`).** The coding function encode assigns its finite list, expression, pair, or tagged value a natural-number index by pairing and list coding.

**Definition (`natBool`).** A natural number decodes to false at zero, true at one, and fails at every larger number.

**Definition (`decodeListBool`).** The decoding operation list bool recovers the finite list, expression, or tagged value represented by its numerical input, returning failure when the code is malformed.

**Definition (`decodeFuel`).** The decoding operation fuel recovers the finite list, expression, or tagged value represented by its numerical input, returning failure when the code is malformed.

**Definition (`SExpr.decode`).** The decoding operation decode recovers the finite list, expression, or tagged value represented by its numerical input, returning failure when the code is malformed.

**Definition (`SsysX`).** An index denotes the decoded nonempty regular language when decoding succeeds and otherwise denotes the universal language.

